    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Designing the missing vector</h1>
      <p>A vision for outcomes, meaning and civic clarity.</p>
    </header>

    <p>The world has chosen its obsession. Every year, nations and companies spend trillions chasing the next technological frontier. Laboratories measure progress in chips, code, molecules, rockets and AI models. Governments build vast funding machines around technology readiness levels and industrial strategy. The future is framed as a race: faster, smaller, smarter machines.</p>

    <p>But something essential is missing. While technology speeds forward on one vector, the other vector, the human side, the meaning side, lags far behind. We pour money into machines and then leave people to fend for themselves, hoping they can catch up, make sense and adapt. This imbalance defines our age: breathtaking technological capability paired with stumbling, fragile and misaligned human systems.</p>

    <p>What if we reversed that imbalance? What if we invested as much rigour, passion and infrastructure in design for meaning, dignity and governance as we do in design for machines? What if human readiness were treated with the same seriousness as technology readiness?</p>

    <p>That is the gap I live in. That is my work.</p>

    <h2>My approach</h2>

    <p>I design for outcomes, repair and civic clarity. My practice refuses to stop at artefacts. It looks at how power, accountability and dignity move through systems, and how design can make those movements visible enough to work on.</p>

    <p>I do this by making systems visible: surfacing hidden assumptions, mapping where responsibility gets lost and showing where harm is displaced.</p>

    <p>I do it by designing for outcomes: shifting attention away from outputs and impact theatre, and towards measurable changes in people’s lives.</p>

    <p>I do it by building connective tools: frameworks, ontologies and provocations that help teams, institutions and movements think together with more clarity.</p>

    <p>And I do it by treating design as civic infrastructure: not decoration, not a final layer, but one of the ways societies organise meaning, decisions and care.</p>

    <p>Sometimes this takes the form of a governance framework like Civic-Needs Governance. Sometimes it is a protest poster. Sometimes it is a custom LLM or an ontology of mind tools for collaboration.</p>

    <p>The form varies. The throughline is the same: design that makes complexity legible, shifts conversations and produces outcomes that matter.</p>

    <h2>What I’m building</h2>

    <p>We are not short on speed. We are short on clarity.</p>

    <p>We are not short on artefacts. We are short on outcomes.</p>

    <p>And we are not short on technology. We are short on the meaning, dignity and governance needed to absorb what technology changes.</p>

    <p>That is where my work sits: building frameworks, tools and provocations that help people see what is happening, name what matters and turn that clarity into better services, decisions and outcomes.</p>

    <p>Technology will keep moving. The question is whether our civic and human systems can move with enough care, evidence and imagination to meet it.</p>

    <p>That is the vector I design for.</p>
